WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.900 --> 00:00:12.820
In May 2024, Google rolled out AI Overviews in Search: an AI-written answer at the top of the page.

2
00:00:13.170 --> 00:00:17.570
Someone asked: the cheese keeps sliding off my pizza. What should I do?

3
00:00:17.920 --> 00:00:22.520
AI Overviews suggested adding some glue to the sauce: 'non-toxic glue will work'.

4
00:00:22.870 --> 00:00:33.030
Reporters traced the idea to a Reddit comment from more than a decade ago, clearly a joke.

5
00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:39.350
Why would AI take a joke seriously? Where do its answers come from?

6
00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:44.973
This is C2: data, models and hallucination.

7
00:00:45.323 --> 00:00:51.403
You'll learn where AI's answers come from, and why it sometimes makes things up with a straight face.

8
00:00:52.200 --> 00:00:59.240
How is an AI made? Four words: data, training, model, answer.

9
00:00:59.590 --> 00:01:06.310
Data is lots of text, images and sound, like the books and exercises a student has read.

10
00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:17.140
Training means letting a program find patterns in the data and adjust again and again, like doing exercises and correcting mistakes. The result is called a model.

11
00:01:17.490 --> 00:01:25.090
When you ask, the model works out an answer from the patterns it learned, like a student applying familiar tricks to a new exam question.

12
00:01:25.440 --> 00:01:32.120
The key point: a model learns patterns. It is not an answer book it can look things up in.

13
00:01:32.900 --> 00:01:40.140
A chat AI learns a special pattern, like a word chain: look at what came before and guess the most likely next word.

14
00:01:40.490 --> 00:01:52.410
This is a demo. Start a famous line of poetry and it finishes it, because that line appears many times in its data, not because it has seen the moon.

15
00:01:52.760 --> 00:02:00.480
At each step there are several plausible words, so the same question can get different answers.

16
00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:10.790
In short: it learns to sound right, not to be right. Usually what sounds right is right, but not always.

17
00:02:11.567 --> 00:02:18.647
Errors that sound true are called hallucinations. OpenAI researchers have explained several causes.

18
00:02:18.997 --> 00:02:27.237
One: the training text isn't labelled true or false, so it learns what sounds plausible.

19
00:02:27.587 --> 00:02:43.107
Two: some facts follow no pattern, like an ordinary person's birthday. Asked for one author's PhD thesis title, a chatbot confidently gave three different answers, all wrong.

20
00:02:43.457 --> 00:02:55.737
Three: many tests only count right answers. A blank scores zero but a guess might be lucky, so models learn to guess when they don't know.

21
00:02:56.534 --> 00:02:59.974
So AI's mistakes usually come from three places.

22
00:03:00.324 --> 00:03:06.044
One: the data was wrong, or a joke, like glue on pizza.

23
00:03:06.394 --> 00:03:14.394
Two: the data has little or nothing on it, like obscure names, dates or sources, so it makes one up.

24
00:03:14.744 --> 00:03:22.264
Three: the data is out of date. The world changed after training, like Everest's latest height.

25
00:03:22.614 --> 00:03:32.214
Even searching the web first isn't foolproof: what it finds may be unreliable, and the writing step can still go wrong.

26
00:03:33.001 --> 00:03:38.001
Here's a challenge: make the AI get it wrong. This is a demo.

27
00:03:38.351 --> 00:03:46.071
Think of an obscure question you can check, like the year a school near you was founded, and ask the AI.

28
00:03:46.421 --> 00:03:51.421
Then check it yourself: the school website, local records, or ask an older neighbour.

29
00:03:51.771 --> 00:04:01.251
If it gives a specific year that doesn't match, you've caught a made-up answer. If it honestly says it doesn't know, note that too.

30
00:04:01.601 --> 00:04:09.001
The point isn't to prove AI is useless, but to see which kinds of questions trip it up.

31
00:04:09.768 --> 00:04:22.168
Three things to remember: AI's answers come from data and a trained model; it learns to sound right, not to be right; check anything obscure, recent or important.

32
00:04:22.518 --> 00:04:30.278
Quick exercise: make an AI get something wrong, and write down its words, your evidence and your conclusion in one line.

33
00:04:30.628 --> 00:04:35.908
How do you check properly? Watch C4: how to tell if what AI says is right.
